   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government in collaboration with the social partners and other relevant stakeholders have advanced sustained peace and social cohesion in Liberia through the promotion of rural employment opportunities and cooperative businesses with a focus on young people in rural areas. In February 202, the Ministries of Labour, Youth and Sports and Gender, Children and Social Protection in collaboration with other relevant stakeholders validated findings of a profile and analysis of youth, gender and land related conflicts in Bong and Lofa Counties. Subsequently, as a result of the recommendations, the capacities of national stakeholders were strengthened in the areas of business development in February 2020 using Start and Improve Your Business (SIYB), cooperative development using ILO training manuals  in August 2021 and access to finance in August 2021. In November 2020, trained trainers conducted training for a total of 85 entrepreneurs at the community-level and thereby finalized their certification as SIYB trainers. The ILO facilitated the development of a &quot;Profile and Analysis of Youth, Gender and Land Related Conflicts&quot; in 2019 to map and document land-, youth- and gender-related drivers of conflicts in the Lofa and Bong counties of Liberia. Findings of the study were presented to national counterparts including government representatives and beneficiaries during a validation meeting organized in February 2020, in Monrovia, Liberia. According to the survey, the counties faced a dual challenge of economic despair and non-inclusive decision-making structures, particularly with regard to land disputes. Similarly, survey respondents named land disputes as the main cause of conflict in their communities (37 per cent), followed by youth unemployment (33 per cent) and youth and women&#x2019;s marginalization (16 per cent).
The ILO supported efforts aimed at addressing these challenges including through a joint project with FAO and WFP funded by the UN Peacebuilding Fund. In early 2020, the ILO facilitated a training of trainers in the Start and Improve Your Business Programme for 16 project partners and youth representatives from Lofa and Bong counties in Gbanga. The training included information on the contribution of employment to peace and social cohesion. Trainer candidates were encouraged to consider social cohesion aspects in the roll out of training activities including through the involvement of different groups. 
A needs assessment of cooperatives and cooperative support organizations conducted in 2020 found that most members of identified cooperatives were above the age of 40, but that young people realized the importance of cooperatives. In August 2021, the ILO supported national counterparts through a training of trainers in cooperative development complementing previous interventions on employment promotion. In August 2021, the ILO also facilitated an online workshop for financial service providers on product diversification.</narrative>
   </description>
